    "created_at": "2022-02-28T05:04:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-02-28T05:11:44Z",
    "body": "While we originally avoided piping to `sh` to avoid [timing attacks](https://www.idontplaydarts.com/2016/04/detecting-curl-pipe-bash-server-side/), I only see one possible attack vector based on the information at that link, and it involves a social aspect:\r\n\r\n  - The server that hosts `starship.rs` gets compromised\r\n  - Person A is suspicious of our code, so she runs `curl`, examines the output, and sends it to `sh`\r\n  - Person B wants to download our code, but is also suspicious of it, so asks person A. Person A says \"oh yeah, I checked it last week, it's fine\"\r\n  - Person B runs `curl | sh`\r\n  - Compromised server detects a `curl | sh` based on timing and injects malicious code.\r\n\r\nIf a user doesn't trust us enough to do `curl | sh` and wants to examine the script themselves, then the modification does not provide any additional protection.\r\n\r\nConversely, if a user trusts us enough to run `curl | sh`, the `sh -c $(curl)` form of the command does not appear to provide any additional protection, since we could just feed them a malicious script in the first place.\r\n\r\nI suppose a user could run `curl`, examine the output, and then `curl | sh`, but...why would you do that?\r\n\r\nAnd finally, as @matchai's link points out, our data is protected in-transit by SSL, so there shouldn't be any shenanigans on that front barring a crypto library bug.\r\n\r\nIn summary, **given that we know we're not intentionally serving malware**, what the `sh -c (curl)` command defends against is a piece of malware infecting `starship.rs` which attempts to serve malware based on `curl | sh` timing attacks[^1]. I suppose this is a possibility, but I don't know if it's a likely enough one to warrant gimping install commands for a sizeable portion of our userbase.\r\n\r\n[^1]: From a user perspective, if they don't trust us, then `sh -c $(curl)` protects against a timing attack. But if they don't trust us, they shouldn't be running the install script without examining it first anyways.",
